        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "31d8dd53-10eb-556f-acc7-d828151cc039",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Sylvia Plath, Michael Cunningham, Ted Hughes, Frieda Hughes, Nicholas Hughes\n- Works: The Bell Jar, The Hours, Specimen Days\n- Places: Boston, Smith College, Newnham College, University of Cambridge, United States, England\n- Topics: poetry, novels, short stories, clinical depression, electroconvulsive therapy\n\nFacts:\n- Sylvia Plath was born on October 27, 1932, in Boston and became an American poet, novelist, and short story writer.\n- Sylvia Plath studied at Smith College and at Newnham College of the University of Cambridge before gaining acclaim as a poet and writer.\n- Sylvia Plath married fellow poet Ted Hughes in 1956, and they lived in the United States and later in England.\n- Sylvia Plath and Ted Hughes had two children, Frieda Hughes and Nicholas Hughes, before separating in 1962.\n- Sylvia Plath was clinically depressed for most of her adult life and received electroconvulsive therapy multiple times.\n- Sylvia Plath wrote The Bell Jar, which was her only novel.\n- Sylvia Plath died by suicide on February 11, 1963.\n- Michael Cunningham was born on November 6, 1952, and is an American novelist and screenwriter.\n- Michael Cunningham wrote The Hours in 1998.\n- The Hours won the Pulitzer Prize for Fiction and the PEN/Faulkner Award in 1999.\n- Michael Cunningham wrote Specimen Days in 2005.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "cbd15173-019b-5b32-b780-a606a6d1a3ce",
        "text": "Page: Sylvia Plath. Sentence: Sylvia Plath ( ; October 27, 1932 – February 11, 1963) was an American poet, novelist, and short story writer.\nPage: Sylvia Plath. Sentence: Born in Boston, she studied at Smith College and Newnham College at the University of Cambridge before receiving acclaim as a poet and writer.\nPage: Sylvia Plath. Sentence: She married fellow poet Ted Hughes in 1956, and they lived together in the United States and then in England.\nPage: Sylvia Plath. Sentence: They had two children, Frieda and Nicholas, before separating in 1962.\nPage: Sylvia Plath. Sentence: Plath was clinically depressed for most of her adult life, and was treated multiple times with electroconvulsive therapy (ECT).\nPage: Sylvia Plath. Sentence: She committed suicide in 1963.\nPage: Michael Cunningham. Sentence: Michael Cunningham (born November 6, 1952) is a U.S. novelist and screenwriter.\nPage: Michael Cunningham. Sentence: He is best known for his 1998 novel \"The Hours\", which won the Pulitzer Prize for Fiction and the PEN/Faulkner Award in 1999.\nPage: The Bell Jar. Sentence: The Bell Jar is the only novel written by the American writer and poet Sylvia Plath.\nPage: Specimen Days. Sentence: Specimen Days is a 2005 novel by American writer Michael Cunningham.",
